  "question": "**Case Study: Computer Graphics — Polygon Lighting and Normal Calculation**<br /><br />A triangular surface in a 3D graphics program has vertices at $A(0,2,3)$, $B(2,5,4)$, and $C(3,3,7)$. A light ray arrives along the vector $\\vec{L} = -\\hat{i} - 2\\hat{j} - 2\\hat{k}$. The surface normal is required for lighting.<br /><br />(a) Find the vectors $\\vec{AB}$ and $\\vec{AC}$.<br /><br />(b) Find a vector normal to the surface using the cross product of $\\vec{AB}$ and $\\vec{AC}$.<br /><br />(c) Calculate a unit normal vector to the triangle.<br /><br />(d) Find the area of the triangle using the normal vector found.<br /><br />(e) Calculate the cosine of the angle between the incident light and the surface normal.<br /><br />(f) If lighting intensity $I$ is proportional to $\\cos \\theta$, estimate $I$ for this triangle if $I_0=100$ units.",
  "answeroption": "B",
  "explanation": "(a)<br /><br />$\\vec{AB} = (2-0)\\hat{i} + (5-2)\\hat{j} + (4-3)\\hat{k} = 2\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j} + 1\\hat{k}$<br /><br />$\\vec{AC} = (3-0)\\hat{i} + (3-2)\\hat{j} + (7-3)\\hat{k} = 3\\hat{i} + 1\\hat{j} + 4\\hat{k}$<br /><br />(b)<br /><br />$\\vec{n} = \\vec{AB} \\times \\vec{AC} = |\\begin{array}{ccc} \\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 \\end{array}|$<br /><br />$= \\hat{i}[(3)(4)-(1)(1)] - \\hat{j}[(2)(4)-(1)(3)] + \\hat{k}[(2)(1)-(3)(3)]$<br /><br />$= \\hat{i}[12-1] - \\hat{j}[8-3] + \\hat{k}[2-9]$<br /><br />$= 11\\hat{i} - 5\\hat{j} - 7\\hat{k}$<br /><br />(c) Magnitude: $|\\vec{n}| = \\sqrt{11^2 + (-5)^2 + (-7)^2} = \\sqrt{121 + 25 + 49} = \\sqrt{195} \\approx 13.96$<br /><br />Unit normal: $\\dfrac{11}{13.96}\\hat{i} - \\dfrac{5}{13.96}\\hat{j} - \\dfrac{7}{13.96}\\hat{k}$<br /><br />(d) Area $= \\dfrac{1}{2}|\\vec{n}| = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\times13.96 = 6.98$ units²<br /><br />(e) $\\vec{L} = -\\hat{i} - 2\\hat{j} - 2\\hat{k}$<br /><br />$|\\vec{L}| = \\sqrt{1 + 4 + 4} = \\sqrt{9} = 3$<br /><br />$\\vec{n} \\cdot \\vec{L} = 11 \\times (-1) + (-5) \\times (-2) + (-7) \\times (-2) = -11 + 10 + 14 = 13$<br /><br />$\\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{13}{13.96 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{13}{41.88} \\approx 0.3105$<br /><br />(f) $I = I_0 \\cos \\theta = 100 \\times 0.3105 = 31.05$ units",
